// Free-range node exhaustion: a release past a full node slab leaks its range
// into the pool's `dropped` counter, and donating `FreeRanges` stops the leak.
//
// Transcript:
//
//   SOS slabnodes: pool trimmed to the construction
//   SOS slabnodes: fragmented holes=32
//   SOS slabnodes: released one more past the node slab
//   SOS slabnodes: donated a page of free ranges
//   SOS slabnodes: the range came back
//   SOS: process fault: argument outside its domain process=...
//
// A pool's free list is made of `FREE_RANGES` nodes. A release that finds none
// leaks its range into the pool's `dropped` counter rather than failing
// (release is infallible). This case builds that path with donations that make
// separators, then donates `FreeRanges` nodes: the leak stops and the pair of
// releases becomes observable.
//
// # The construction
//
// A node is spent only on a hole (a free range with allocated bytes on both
// sides), so exhausting `MAX_FREE_RANGES` nodes needs that many simultaneous
// holes and therefore one more simultaneous separator. Two walls make the
// obvious program impossible:
//
// 1. A separator has to be bytes the pool has handed out and not got back. A
//    live region is one, and a process cannot name that many (`MAX_HANDLES` is
//    smaller). `MAX_MEMORIES` is not the binding wall; raising it alone would
//    not help.
//
// 2. Cutting the holes one at a time does not work either, because `pool_cut`
//    is first fit: the next cut would be served out of the hole just made
//    instead of from the cursor, and the fragmentation would never grow.
//
// Both are answered by the op under test. A donated region's bytes leave the
// pool permanently and its Memory slot is retired, so a donation is a separator
// that costs no handle and no slot, which is what lets them all fit. And the
// holes are cut in strictly growing sizes, so no later cut can be served out of
// an earlier hole and every one comes off the cursor.
//
// # Why this case is riscv32 only
//
// Growing sizes are forced by first fit, and the step (`STEP`) is a few bytes,
// a small multiple of `hal.PROT_GRAIN` here; the grain is a whole page on the
// other profile. With a page step the holes alone would want `PROT_GRAIN * (1 +
// 2 + ... + HOLES)` bytes, and their separators as much again, against a pool
// that cannot grow (the other profile's grant window caps it). The construction
// is arithmetically impossible there.
//
// # How the leak is observed, given that `dropped` has no op
//
// By a controlled pair: two identical releases of two identical regions, one
// before the `FreeRanges` donation and one after.
//
//   R1 is released with the node slab full. It leaks; its bytes are gone.
//   R2 is released after nodes have been donated. It gets a node.
//
// Then `split` is the read-out. The pool's cursor is spent to zero by
// construction, and every remaining hole is smaller than R2, so a split of R2's
// size can only be served out of R2's own returned range: it succeeds, and "the
// range came back" is that success.
//
// The fault at the end is the other half of the pair. Asking for the same size
// once more can only be served by R1's range, which leaked, so `pool_can_serve`
// says no and `MemoryOp.Split` ends the caller with `BadArg` (a caller that
// asks a pool for bytes it does not have could have checked). The case
// therefore expects root to fault, as `tests/slab-donate-shared` does. A kernel
// that had not leaked R1 would have served that split and the run would end
// cleanly, so the fault is the assertion.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, SlabKind, SosStatus}

/// The runner's pool length for this profile. Mirrored here because the
/// construction has to spend the cursor to exactly zero: `split` faults when a
/// pool cannot serve the ask, so the program may never over-request.
static POOL_LEN: UInt = 0x40000

/// Holes to make, which is `MAX_FREE_RANGES`: the node slab, filled exactly.
static HOLES: Int = 32

/// The first hole's size, and the step every later one grows by. The step is
/// a small multiple of the protection grain on this profile; it must be
/// positive (see the header).
static HOLE0: UInt = 256
static STEP: UInt = 8

/// A separator is cut just after the hole below it and must be larger than
/// every hole that exists, so first fit cannot serve it out of one.
static SEP_BUMP: UInt = 4

/// The last three separators are also `SlabKind.Threads` donations, and a
/// thread unit is a slot plus a frame stride, so they are sized for that rather
/// than for the four bytes the layout needs.
static BIG_SEP: UInt = 1536

/// The controlled pair's size. Larger than every hole, so a split of it can
/// only be served out of one of these two ranges.
static PAIR: UInt = 512

/// The page donated to `FreeRanges` between the two releases.
static NODES: UInt = 4096

/// `SlabKind.Events`, the first separator kind. Named because a bare `0` does
/// not adopt a platform `UInt` at an argument.
static EVENTS_KIND: UInt = 0

/// `SlabKind.Threads`'s raw value: the kind the three big separators go to.
static THREADS_KIND: UInt = 9

/// Kinds 0..7 (`Events` .. `Mappings`) take four separators each, which is
/// `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS` per kind and exactly the number the small separators
/// need. `SlabKind.Memories` is one of them.
static SEPS_PER_KIND: Int = 4

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// Hole `i`'s length.
func hole_len(i: Int) -> UInt { HOLE0 + STEP * (i as UInt) }

/// The separator cut just below hole `i`: bigger than hole `i - 1`, the largest
/// free range in existence when it is cut.
func sep_len(i: Int) -> UInt { hole_len(i) + SEP_BUMP }

/// Every byte the construction spends, so the trim below can leave exactly that
/// much on the cursor.
func construction_bytes() -> UInt {
    var total: UInt = NODES + PAIR + PAIR + 3 * BIG_SEP
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < HOLES {
        total = total + hole_len(i) + sep_len(i)
        i = i + 1
    }
    total
}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var pool_record = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: no pool region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let pool = pool_record.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: pool record is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // The read-out at the end depends on the cursor being spent, so that a
    // split can only be served out of a returned range. The surplus goes where
    // every separator goes, into a slab permanently, and `Pipes` takes it
    // because a pipe unit is the largest in the kernel and one extent swallows
    // the lot.
    let surplus = POOL_LEN - construction_bytes()
    let trim = try pool.split(len: surplus) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: trim split failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try system.slab_donate(kind: SlabKind.Pipes, memory: move trim) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: trim donation refused\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: pool trimmed to the construction\n")

    // The nodes for stage 2, cut before the fragmentation so that the pool is
    // whole when it is taken and the construction's arithmetic stays exact.
    let nodes_page = try pool.split(len: NODES) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: nodes split failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // `sep, hole, sep, hole, ...`: each hole is released only once the
    // separator above it is out of the pool, so it touches neither a free
    // neighbour nor the cursor and must take a node of its own.
    guard let first_kind = SlabKind.from(raw: EVENTS_KIND) else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: kind 0 is not a kind\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let first_sep = try pool.split(len: sep_len(0)) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: first separator failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try system.slab_donate(kind: first_kind, memory: move first_sep) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: first separator refused\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    var made: Int = 0
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < HOLES {
        let hole = try pool.split(len: hole_len(i)) catch {
            print("SOS slabnodes: hole {} failed: {}\n", i, error)
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        }

        // The separator above this hole. Its kind is the next extent in the
        // round-robin; the last one is a Threads extent, which is why it is
        // big.
        let next = i + 1
        let raw = if next < HOLES { (next / SEPS_PER_KIND) as UInt } else { THREADS_KIND }
        let want = if next < HOLES { sep_len(next) } else { BIG_SEP }
        guard let kind = SlabKind.from(raw: raw) else {
            print("SOS slabnodes: {} is not a kind\n", raw)
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        }
        let sep = try pool.split(len: want) catch {
            print("SOS slabnodes: separator {} failed: {}\n", next, error)
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        }
        try system.slab_donate(kind: kind, memory: move sep) catch {
            print("SOS slabnodes: separator {} refused\n", next)
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        }

        // The release that makes the hole. Both neighbours are donated bytes,
        // so there is nothing to coalesce with and nothing to fold into.
        let _ = move hole
        made = made + 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    print("SOS slabnodes: fragmented holes={}\n", made)

    // ---- R1: the release that leaks ---------------------------------------
    let r1 = try pool.split(len: PAIR) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: R1 split failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let threads_kind = SlabKind.from(raw: THREADS_KIND) else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: Threads is not a kind\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let sep_r1 = try pool.split(len: BIG_SEP) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: R1 separator failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try system.slab_donate(kind: threads_kind, memory: move sep_r1) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: R1 separator refused\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    // The node slab holds exactly `HOLES` live nodes at this point, so this
    // release finds none and the range is added to the pool's `dropped` and
    // lost.
    let _ = move r1
    let _ = system.debug_print(
        "SOS slabnodes: released one more past the node slab\n")

    // ---- R2: the same release, held back ----------------------------------
    let r2 = try pool.split(len: PAIR) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: R2 split failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let sep_r2 = try pool.split(len: BIG_SEP) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: R2 separator failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try system.slab_donate(kind: threads_kind, memory: move sep_r2) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: R2 separator refused\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // ---- Stage 2: donate the nodes ----------------------------------------
    try system.slab_donate(kind: SlabKind.FreeRanges, memory: move nodes_page) catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: node donation refused\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: donated a page of free ranges\n")

    // The identical release, now with a node to take. Its range goes onto the
    // free list where R1's went nowhere.
    let _ = move r2

    // The cursor is spent and every hole is smaller than `PAIR`, so this can
    // only be served out of R2's returned range.
    let back = try pool.split(len: PAIR) catch {
        print("SOS slabnodes: the range did NOT come back: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabnodes: the range came back\n")

    // The other half of the pair, which is the assertion.
    //
    // `back` is still held, so R2's range is spent and the only thing left in
    // the machine that could serve another `PAIR` is R1's range. It leaked, so
    // `pool_can_serve` says no and `MemoryOp.Split` ends this process with
    // `BadArg`. Had R1's release found a node, this split would have been
    // served out of it and the run would have ended cleanly, which is what
    // makes the fault a measurement of the leak rather than of the pool.
    let _ = pool.split(len: PAIR)
    let _ = system.debug_print(
        "SOS slabnodes: UNREACHABLE — the leaked range was served\n")
    let _ = move back
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
